<spell-notebook "code usage">
	<header level="1">
		Code
		<header>Code shows a block of source code coloured by its language, from the element's own text or a file on
              this site.</>
	</>
	<code-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#languages">Languages</>
				<ui-item link="#events">Events</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  from the old Astro page's Behaviour, plus the vocabulary's events and the shadow markup's a11y. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write the code inside `<ui-code>` and say its `language`. The text comes from, first match wins:

			- `source`: a file of this site (same origin only), fetched when `load` says (`eager`, `visible` or `idle`).
			- A `<script type="text/plain">` child: its text exactly, so `<` and `&` need no escaping.
			- The element's own text, dedented, so it can be indented with the markup around it.

			The `content` property replaces either until `source` changes. Leave `language` out to have it guessed among the
			common languages (`detectedLanguage` says which), or say `text` for no colours at all.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Languages">
		<markdown>
			highlight.js loads lazily, with the first code shown; until it arrives, the code shows plain.

			`UI.code.register(name, language)` adds a language: a highlight.js grammar, a highlighter of your own returning
			`{ start, end, kind }` spans, or a `load()` that fetches either on first use. Elements already showing that
			language colour again. An unknown language shows plain text and fires a `ui-error` (`kind: "render"`), with no
			message: the code is still there.

			Spell itself is such a language, from outside Spell UI: the app's `<spell-code>` registers it, so on a page
			that loads it, `language="spell"` is coloured here too.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			UI.code.register("ini", {
			  load: async () => ({ grammar: (await import("highlight.js/lib/languages/ini")).default })
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-coloured` -- coloured: `{ language, detected }`, `language` `undefined` for plain text, `detected` when it
			  was guessed.
			- `ui-copied` -- the copy button copied `{ content }`.
			- `ui-loaded` -- the text arrived, from `source` or the element's own content: `{ source, content }`.
			- `ui-error` (cancelable) -- loading or colouring failed: `{ kind, source, error }`. Cancel it to skip the error
			  message.
			- `ui-changed`, `ui-saving`, `ui-saved` -- editing and saving, shared with `<ui-markdown>` and `<ui-include>` (see
			  Include's Usage tab).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const code = document.querySelector("ui-code")
			code.addEventListener("ui-coloured", (event) => console.log(event.detail.language, event.detail.detected))
			code.addEventListener("ui-copied", (event) => console.log("copied", event.detail.content.length, "characters"))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- With `copy`, **Tab** reaches the copy button first; **Enter** or **Space** copies.
			- **Tab** then reaches the code itself (the `<pre>` is a tab stop), so the arrow keys can scroll a long line
			  sideways.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The code is a real `<pre><code>`, named by its language as written ("ts code", else "Code"), so a screen reader
			  says what it is before reading it.
			- The copy button says "Copy", then "Copied" for a moment (both translatable).
			- Colours are never the only signal: the code reads the same without them.
			- While `source` loads, a `<ui-loader>` (`role="status"`) says so; a failed load shows a `<ui-message>` saying
			  why.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
